[claude-9b] Released in setup tool 2.23.0 (2026-10-28), tested by claude-9b-test in s157 (run G) and s157c3 (mp_merv
4 players: the broken first frames gone). The game's side is in recomp/src/game/cheat_watch.cpp (cod3_level_start_hold,
on). The text below and the "UNTESTED" marks in the code comments are from the draft.
[claude-9b-think] 2026-10-27 draft. SDK patch 0149: hold slow presents just after a level
loads (the game's side: level-start-hold-game.UNTESTED.diff, cod3_level_start_hold, default off). Owner 19:31:
"optimizting in-game performance and reducing/eliminating hitching is priority #1".

Why (the owner's play today): the first frame after "loaded in" makes most of the level's textures, uploads and
render targets on the GPU thread: saint_lo 95.9 ms (273 textures made 44.1 ms, 65 MB uploaded 20.0 ms, resolves
17.8 ms), nightdrop 121.8 ms (307 made 56.9 ms, 72 MB 23.3 ms, resolves 23.2 ms), mp_hosta 58.4 ms (217 made
36.6 ms). The loading picture is up until that frame is presented anyway; what can be seen is the step between
the first two shown frames: the game's next frame moves that whole time at once, often during the level's fade
from black.

What it does:
  - gpu_stats (REX_GRAPHICS_STATS_VERSION 10): HoldSlowPresentsFor(window_ms, slow_ms), called by the game at a
    level's load end; HoldThisPresent(), called by each backend's IssueSwap once a swap (the GPU thread): it
    keeps the time of the swap, and inside the window returns the time since the previous swap when that is
    over slow_ms (the picture is then held, counted); PresentsHeld(), the total.
  - Vulkan and D3D12 IssueSwap, right after the swap's BeginSubmission: a held picture ends the submission
    without presenting (Vulkan's own path for a frame that used placeholders, command_processor.cpp 2606-2620;
    D3D12 ends a frame without presenting the same way, its line 2265-2267), and logs one line:
      "Presentation held at a level's start: a 95.9 ms frame not shown (1 so far)"
    The picture before it stays (the loading screen or black); the first one shown is the next, normal frame.
    The frame's commands are submitted as usual; the game's own timing is unchanged (so hitch-counts' "since
    the level loaded" still counts the slow first frame: it measures the game thread).
  - A run of slow frames in the window is held too (at most the window, 2 s with the game's call); a swap
    after a stall (no swaps for a while) is held once.
Not changed: what the first frame does (making the textures earlier would need to know which ones; no cheap
version), map_restart (checkpoint restarts: not measured; the game diff calls it at SV_SpawnServer only).

Test (s157 or later; game diff applied; FINDINGS-HITCHES): saint_lo and nightdrop from the menu, and one MP map,
each with --cod3_level_start_hold=false and =true. Capture the first second after the level appears (a screen
recording at 60 fps or more, or REA's capture tool), and read the log: per level start, 1-2 "Presentation
held at a level's start" lines with =true, none with =false, none during play. Expect: with =true no jump
between the first two frames shown; the level appears about one slow frame later than the loading screen
would otherwise have been replaced (the same moment the slow frame would have been shown).

